## 🔴 The partner question, settled before any measurement (finding O-20)

  The queue left it genuinely open whether swi_b (Base licence, holds `L3-MC-ROUTE` but no
  `PIM` bit) can be a PIM neighbour.  Settled by sending `service pim` ALONE on swi_b, then
  `service pdm` alone (x960.md 7A.1: the start is asynchronous and `service`/`router` must not
  share a batch):

    x960-28YSQ(config)#service pim
    % Service is not currently licensed on this platform
    x960-28YSQ(config)#service pdm
    % Service is not currently licensed on this platform

  Both refused, so **nothing was applied to swi_b** (verified: `show running-config` before and
  after holds only `service password-encryption` and `service ssh`).  Note swi_b's `service ?`
  list DOES offer `pdm`, `pim` and `pim6`, identically to the other two units, so the read-only
  surface probe TRIAGE ran could never have settled this.  => the PIM partner is **swi_a**, used
  under RISK-1: configured freely, never reloaded, never power-cycled.
  Recorded in the queue as **O-20**.

## The topology this case runs on (group C's shared setup)

  Both usable host NICs land on the DUT, which the queue read as "no host behind a partner".
  Triage's design defeats that, and it is what makes an inter-router PIM test possible here: a
  NIC's PHYSICAL port and its ROUTING router are independent.

    host eth3 10.38.22.33/27 -(DUT port1.0.10 access vlan 10, SVI 10.38.22.34/27 on the DUT)- swi_d
                                                                                               ||
                                        DUT port1.0.4 TRUNK, tagged vlan 48 + vlan 61           ||
                                                                                               ||
    host eth4 10.38.22.65/27 -(DUT port1.0.48 access vlan 48, NO SVI on the DUT)================||
                                                                                               ||
                                                                                             swi_a
                                                      vlan 48 SVI 10.38.22.66/27  (receiver's router, DR)
                                                      vlan 61 SVI 10.0.61.2/30    (transit, PIM adjacency, and the RP)

  - DUT = the source's first-hop router / DR on vlan 10.  swi_a = the receiver's last-hop router
    / DR on vlan 48, **and the RP** (`ip pim rp-address 10.0.61.2` on both).  The RP was put on
    the PARTNER on purpose: with the RP on the DUT the source would be directly attached to the
    RP and the Register path would not exist to be measured.
  - `vlan 61` 10.0.61.0/30 has no host NIC, so every control-plane claim on it is read with the
    DUT's own `tcpdump` at privileged exec (the queue's techniques block).
  - Routes: DUT `ip route 10.38.22.64/27 10.0.61.2`; swi_a `ip route 10.38.22.32/27 10.0.61.1`.
  - 🔴 The shared underlay's `interface vlan48 / ip address 10.38.22.66/27` on the DUT is
    REMOVED for the whole of group C and the address moved to swi_a.  `no interface vlan48` is
    **refused on AW+** (`% Removal of interface not allowed`), so the address was taken off with
    `no ip address` and the SVI left in place with no L3 — same effect, and recorded in the
