## Measurement stance (STANDING-ORDERS 7)

  Instrument: `tools/arpleg.py` — starts tcpdump on every NIC named, sends a known count of
  crafted frames, stops, decodes and prints a grouped count WITH the Ethernet source and
  destination of every flow.  Every leg below carries, in the SAME leg:
   - the **subject** group;
   - a **control group that was never joined**, which must NOT arrive;
   - an **instrument control** — a routed ICMP unicast 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65 that must
     always arrive — so a zero is a measurement and not a dead capture.
  The instrument control arrived at its full count in **every one of the 9 counted legs**, and
  it arrives with **eth src 88:9d:98:df:26:02 = swi_a's MAC**, which is itself the proof that
  the two-router path is real: the frame was routed by the DUT, crossed the trunk, and was
  routed again by the partner before reaching eth4.

  🔴 **A generic extension to `arpleg.py` was needed and made** (not a case-local script):
  `--l2-dst` is a single value, so one leg could not carry two multicast groups each with its
  correct link-layer address.  `arpleg.py` now derives the IANA address for an IPv4 multicast
  `--udp` destination itself (RFC 1112 6.4, `01:00:5e` + the low 23 bits) when no `--l2-dst` is
  given; an explicit `--l2-dst` still wins, so a deliberately MISMATCHED L2 address stays
  testable.  Verified in the transcripts below: 239.1.1.1 -> 01:00:5e:01:01:01 and
  239.9.9.9 -> 01:00:5e:09:09:09 in the same leg.  README entry updated.
  Receiver side: `tools/igmp.py`, extended in this group with an IGMPv3 mode for T43865; this
  case used its IGMPv2 path only.

