## LEG 4 (designed) — the PIM-SM mechanism itself, decoded on the transit wire

  vlan 61 has no host NIC, so this used the DUT's own tcpdump at privileged exec, with a fresh
  group (239.2.2.2) joined first so the whole tree build could be watched from cold:

    awplus#tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 18 -nn
    03:43:38.943301 IP 10.0.61.1    > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Hello, length 34
    03:43:39.659544 IP 10.38.22.34  > 10.0.61.2 : PIMv2, Register, length 62
    03:43:39.660110 IP 10.0.61.2    > 224.0.0.13: PIMv2, Join / Prune, length 34
    03:43:39.999623 IP 10.38.22.40.40000 > 239.2.2.2.5001: UDP, length 26
    03:43:39.999965 IP 10.38.22.34  > 10.0.61.2 : PIMv2, Register, length 62
    18 packets captured
    (far side, same leg: 18 of 20 subject frames on eth4, 20/20 instrument control)

  Read in order: the DUT (10.38.22.34 — its DR address on the SOURCE interface, not its transit
  address) unicasts a **Register** to the RP; **0.6 ms later** the RP sends a **Join/Prune**
  toward the source; **340 ms after that** the group's data is flowing NATIVELY on vlan 61.
  That is the complete PIM-SM shared-tree-to-SPT transition, measured, not inferred.

  Repeated with a PIM-only filter and a third group (239.3.3.3) to catch the end of the
  handshake:

    awplus#tcpdump -i vlan61 -c 10 -nn proto 103
    03:44:53.378341 IP 10.0.61.2   > 224.0.0.13 : PIMv2, Hello, length 34
    03:44:54.283754 IP 10.38.22.34 > 10.0.61.2  : PIMv2, Register, length 28     <- NULL register
    03:44:54.284280 IP 10.0.61.2   > 10.38.22.34: PIMv2, Register Stop, length 18
    03:45:02.675409 IP 10.38.22.34 > 10.0.61.2  : PIMv2, Register, length 66     <- data register
    03:45:02.676041 IP 10.0.61.2   > 224.0.0.13 : PIMv2, Join / Prune, length 34
    03:45:03.011682 IP 10.38.22.34 > 10.0.61.2  : PIMv2, Register, length 66
    03:45:03.354785 IP 10.38.22.34 > 10.0.61.2  : PIMv2, Register, length 66
    03:45:03.355179 IP 10.0.61.2   > 10.38.22.34: PIMv2, Register Stop, length 18
    03:45:08.943538 IP 10.0.61.1   > 224.0.0.13 : PIMv2, Hello, length 34
    03:45:17.064200 IP 10.38.22.34 > 10.0.61.2  : PIMv2, Register, length 28     <- null keepalive
    (far side, same leg: 38 of 40 subject frames on eth4, 40/40 instrument control)

  => Register (28 bytes = null, 62/66 bytes = data-encapsulated), RP Join toward the source,
     **Register-Stop once the SPT is up**, and the null-register keepalive afterwards.  Every
     element of RFC 4601's Register state machine that this topology can exercise, observed.
  Resulting tables, with two trees up at once:
    swi_a#show ip mroute
    (10.38.22.40, 239.2.2.2), uptime 00:02:19  Owner PIM-SM, Flags: TF
      Incoming interface: vlan61        Outgoing interface list: vlan48 (1)
    (10.38.22.40, 239.3.3.3), uptime 00:00:56  Owner PIM-SM, Flags: TF
      Incoming interface: vlan61        Outgoing interface list: vlan48 (1)
  VERDICT leg 4: PASS.

