## Teardown and its proof

  This case's own additions were removed from BOTH devices; group C's shared topology (STP
  priority, vlan 61, the trunk, the two shut ports, the vlan61 SVI, the static routes, and the
  DUT's vlan48 address being absent) is GROUP setup and is deliberately left up for T43864/65/66
  — it is written into the queue with its exact restore recipe.

    DUT   : no ip pim sparse-mode (vlan10, vlan61), no ip pim rp-address, no ip multicast-routing,
            then `no service pim` sent alone
    swi_a : no ip pim sparse-mode (vlan48, vlan61), no ip igmp, no ip pim rp-address,
            no ip multicast-routing, then `no service pim` sent alone

  `no service pim` answered on BOTH units with
    % Save the config and restart for this change to take effect.
  (an advisory, not a rejection — `cfg.py` stops at it, which is harmless here because it was
  the last command of its batch; worth knowing for any batch that continues past it).

  `python3 tools/rcdiff.py work/pre-u2.log work/post-u2.log` and the same for u5
  (**gated on the OUTPUT — `rcdiff.py` exits 0 even when it prints differences**):

  - **swi_a**: the ONLY differences are group C's shared topology (vlan 48 + vlan 61, the
    port1.0.4 trunk, the two SVIs, the static route).  **No PIM residue of any kind.**
  - **DUT**: group C's shared topology, plus ONE extra line:  `no service pim`.

  🔴 That pair reproduces x960.md 7A.2 exactly, side by side, in the same minute, with the same
  commands: the sticky `no service <proto>` line appears **only on the unit that BOOTED with the
  service**.  The DUT booted from `flash:/ck43863.cfg`, which contained `service pim`, during
  leg 7; swi_a never rebooted at all.  Configuration cannot remove the line.  It is cleared by
  the group-end reload of the DUT onto `flash:/default.cfg`, which is in the queue's restore
  recipe and is the reason that reload is not optional.  Semantically it is the default (no
  process, no daemon), so it is cosmetic — but it is a real running-config difference and it is
  reported here rather than hidden.
  swi_b: nothing to tear down; its two `service` commands were refused and nothing was applied.

## Observations worth carrying (full text in the queue's `## Issues`)

  - **F-13** — `ip pim sparse-mode` does not enable router-side IGMP; snooping shows the group
    anyway and PIM never sees it.  Leg 2.
  - **O-20** — `service <proto>` gives a clean one-line licence verdict where the feature's own
    `show` blames daemon-or-licence.  Settled the partner question in two minutes.
  - **O-21** — `show ip igmp groups` reports `Last Reporter 0.0.0.0` on this build even when the
    report on the wire carries a correct on-subnet source (10.38.22.70, confirmed by capture),
    and it does so both before and after `ip igmp` was enabled.  Cosmetic, but it cost
    diagnosis time in leg 2 because it looks like a malformed report.
  - **O-22** — `no interface vlanN` is refused on AW+ (`% Removal of interface not allowed`);
    an SVI is retired with `no ip address`, and the VLAN itself with `no vlan N`.
  - **O-23** — a tool defect found and fixed in `tools/console.py`; see below.

## Tools used, with the exact arguments

  - `tools/tb` for EVERY step on the box (Ask-CK D6).  Scratch `/tmp/ckt1406c/`.
  - `tools/ckcase.py` — `tools/tb tool tb277 ckt1406c ckcase AWPTCM-T43863` (no authored text).
  - `tools/arpleg.py` via `tools/tb sudotool` — all 9 counted legs, arguments quoted in each leg.
    **Extended in this case**: derives the IANA L2 address for an IPv4 multicast `--udp`
    destination (README updated).
  - `tools/igmp.py` via `tools/tb sudotool` — IGMPv2 joins/leaves, arguments quoted in each leg.
  - `tools/cfg.py`, `tools/ckcon.py`, `tools/ckyn.py`, `tools/qmark.py`, `tools/ckreload.py`,
    `tools/rc2cfg.py`, `tools/rcdiff.py` — all via `tools/tb`.
  - the DUT's own `tcpdump -i vlan61 -c N -nn [proto 103]` at privileged exec, for leg 4.

  🔴 **TOOL DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED — `tools/console.py` (O-23).**  AW+ port-state messages are
  written to the console by the switch driver itself, NOT through the logging subsystem, so
  neither `terminal no monitor` nor `no log console` suppresses them (both measured here).  One
  can land INSIDE the prompt:
      shutdown
      switch: port 14(port1.0.14) entered disabled state
      awplus(config-ifswitch: port 14(port1.0.14) entered disabled state
      )#
  and a second family does the same on `ip pim sparse-mode`:
      awplus(config-ifvlan61: entered allmulticast mode
      )#
  Neither half is a prompt — `)#` does not start with `[\w.-]` — so the command ran to its FULL
  90 s timeout and `cfg.py` then **silently abandoned every command after it**, although the
  command itself had taken effect.  It cost two truncated batches here (and would have cost one
  per `shutdown`/`no shutdown` for the rest of the campaign) before it was fixed.
  Fixed generically, two ways so an unseen message still works: `PROMPT_ANYWHERE_RE` now also
  matches a line that begins `)#`, which is the tail a splice always leaves and which no device
  output legitimately produces; and `SPLICE_RE`/`unsplice()` strip the known message families
  from a COPY of the buffer and retry.  The transcript and the returned output keep every byte.
  Self-tested on 6 buffers (both splice families, two normal prompts, the pre-existing trailing
  splice, and a negative control) before being pushed to the box, and then used for the rest of
  this case with no further stalls.

